==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|dodge|-y|pos1=verb|id2=adjectival}}. First used in mid-19th century England.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɒd͡ʒ.i/}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 dodgy.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒdʒi|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}} {{tlb|en|colloquial}}

# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Commonwealth}} {{U|evasive}} and [[shifty]].
#: {{ux|en|Asked why, a spokesman gave a '''dodgy''' answer about legal ramifications.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Commonwealth}} {{U|unsound}} and [[unreliable]].
#: {{ux|en|Never listen to '''dodgy''' advice.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''dodgy''' old machine kept breaking down.}}
# {{U|dishonest}}.
#: {{ux|en|The more money the better, because there is always that '''dodgy''' politician or corrupt official to bribe.}}
#: {{ux|en|I am sure you wouldn't want to be seen buying '''dodgy''' gear, would you? (stolen goods).}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2010-11-10|author=Pratish Mistry|title=Why Julius Malema is important to the ANC|work=NewsTime
|passage=No doubt you’re tired of reading about his colourful escapades – about his glamorous home, his allegedly '''dodgy''' tenderpreneur deals, his gushing praise of malevolent dictators, or his soundly articulated international economic policy.}}
# {{U|risky}}.
#: {{ux|en|This is a slightly '''dodgy''' plan, because there is a lot that is being changed for this fix.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Louise Story|title=F.T.C. to Review Online Ads and Privacy|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/11/01/technology/01Privacy.html?_r=1|date=2007-11-01|passage=“The market is getting edgier and edgier, and what is accepted in the marketplace gets '''dodgier''' and '''dodgier''',” said Martin E. Abrams, the executive director of the Center for Information Policy Leadership at the law firm Hunton & Williams, a research organization financed by companies like Google, Microsoft and Best Buy.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230419174820/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/11/01/technology/01Privacy.html?_r=1|archivedate=19 April 2023}}
# {{U|deviant}}.
#: {{ux|en|He's a '''dodgy''' Peeping Tom.}}
# {{U|uncomfortable}} and [[weird]].
#: {{ux|en|The situation was right '''dodgy'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm feeling '''dodgy''' today, probably got the flu.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Burkhard Bilger|title=Fortune Hunters and Gatherers|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/10/27/books/fortune-hunters-and-gatherers.html|date=2002-10-27|passage=Churchill's own forefathers were among the '''dodgiest''' of the lot -- one a spendthrift, another a child molester, a third so reclusive that he went three years without saying a word.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210214004435/https://www.nytimes.com/2002/10/27/books/fortune-hunters-and-gatherers.html|archivedate=14 February 2021}}

